Errors of misuse (unnamed task arguments, out-of-range slots, operations
on a closed handle) stay plain errors: the classed hierarchy covers the
outcomes that a running system produces, not programming mistakes.

Raised \code{sora_error} conditions are distinct from sentinels (class
\code{sora_sentinel}, returned by \code{\link[=sora_send]{sora_send()}}, \code{\link[=sora_recv]{sora_recv()}} and
\code{\link[=sora_collect]{sora_collect()}}). A sentinel is an ordinary return value that tags a
terminal state on the hot path, not a signalled condition. See
\code{\link[=sora_is_sentinel]{sora_is_sentinel()}}.

Which discipline applies follows the shape of the call. The verbs that
move payloads and wait with a bound — \code{\link[=sora_send]{sora_send()}}, \code{\link[=sora_recv]{sora_recv()}},
\code{\link[=sora_collect]{sora_collect()}}, \code{\link[=sora_map]{sora_map()}} — return sentinels for transport states.
These are: not yet (\code{sora_timeout}), not now (\code{sora_full}), stream over
(\code{sora_closed}, \code{sora_peer_gone}). Their caller is a loop, and these are
its normal outcomes. Conditions are raised where a request failed for
good. Constructors and \code{\link[=sora_submit]{sora_submit()}}, whose return is a handle the
next line uses, raise on every failure. \code{\link[=sora_collect]{sora_collect()}} raises when the
value can never arrive: the own error of the task re-signalled,
\code{sora_error_cancelled}, \code{sora_error_worker_died}. A sentinel invites the
next iteration of the loop. A condition means stop and deal with it.
